  <p class="eyebrow">Literature summary · four published studies · no page numbers, because this is not the manual</p>
  <h1>Nuclear winter, from the 1983 paper to the 2019 model runs</h1>

  <p class="lede" id="model-studies-note">
    <strong>These are published model studies, not figures from the 1977 manual.
    Nothing on this page is our own calculation.</strong>
    Every number below carries the paper it came from. Where the manual does have something to
    say — the physics of the fallout itself, Chapter 9 — it is in a section of its own, with
    printed page numbers, and it is not mixed into the model results.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-means">What the term means</h2>
  <p>
    Nuclear winter is the name given to the global cooling that follows a large nuclear war, and
    the mechanism is smoke, not blast. Fires started by air bursts in cities lift soot into the
    stratosphere, where rain cannot wash it out; the smoke absorbs sunlight above the surface and
    cools the ground below it. The 1983 paper that coined the term simulated exchanges of several
    thousand megatons and found that dust and smoke would encircle the Earth within 1 to 2 weeks,
    reducing average light levels to a few percent of normal and taking land temperatures to
    &minus;15 to &minus;25&nbsp;°C (Turco 1983). None of that is in the 1977 manual, which stops
    at the effects of a single explosion.
  </p>

  <h2 id="1983">1983: the first quantitative claim</h2>
  <p>
    The 1983 result was not only about an all-out exchange. Roughly 100 megatons detonated over
    major urban centres was enough to push hemispheric smoke optical depth above 2 for weeks and
    to keep land subfreezing for months even in summer (Turco 1983). The same paper recorded a
    second, separate hazard: in a 5,000-megaton war, fallout at northern mid-latitude sites far
    from any target could deliver chronic mean whole-body gamma doses of up to 50 rads over days
    to weeks (Turco 1983). These were model runs built on atmospheric models first used for
    volcanic eruptions, and the authors were explicit that the results were imprecise — but that
    the first-order effects were serious.
  </p>

  <h2 id="2007">2007: the same question, a modern model</h2>
  <p>
    Twenty years later the question was asked again with a model that reached 80&nbsp;km and ran
    for ten years. Robock and colleagues simulated two loadings, 50 and 150&nbsp;Tg of smoke,
    using moderate and large shares of the arsenal that existed at the time. Both produced
    significant climatic responses; the 150&nbsp;Tg case still qualified as nuclear winter; and
    the response was more long-lasting than the earlier work suggested, because the model lifted
    the plume into the middle and upper stratosphere, where aerosol lifetimes are measured in
    years rather than weeks (Robock 2007). The finding was not that 1983 had overstated the
    danger, but that it had understated how long the danger would persist.
  </p>

  <h2 id="2019-regional">2019: a regional war is enough</h2>
  <p>
    A regional exchange, not a superpower war: India using 100 strategic weapons against
    Pakistani cities and Pakistan using 150. Fatalities could reach 50 to 125 million, and fires
    could release 16 to 36&nbsp;Tg of black carbon. That smoke would cut surface sunlight by 20
    to 35 percent, cool the global surface by 2 to 5&nbsp;°C, reduce precipitation by 15 to 30
    percent, and take more than ten years to clear; net primary productivity would fall 15 to 30
    percent on land and 5 to 15 percent in the oceans (Toon 2019). The authors put both arsenals
    at 400 to 500 weapons by 2025, with yields from 12 to 45&nbsp;kt up to a few hundred
    kilotons.
  </p>

  <h2 id="2019-global">2019: and in a second model</h2>
  <p>
    Coupe and colleagues ran the full United States–Russia case, 150&nbsp;Tg of soot, in WACCM4
    at 2° resolution with 66 layers reaching 140&nbsp;km, and compared it with the 2007 ModelE
    run. The two models differ in detail and agree on the outcome: WACCM4 removes smoke more
    quickly and still produces larger temperature and precipitation reductions than ModelE, with
    below-freezing temperatures over much of the Northern Hemisphere in summer (Coupe 2019). Two
    generations of model, twelve years apart, land on nuclear winter for the same smoke loading.
    These are the only two comprehensive climate-model simulations of that scenario published so
    far.
  </p>

  <h2 id="manual">What the 1977 manual can and cannot say</h2>
  <p>
    The manual predates all of this and cannot check it: it has no climate model and no soot.
    What it has is the physics of fallout. Early, or local, fallout is defined as the material
    reaching the ground during the first 14 hours after the explosion, with surface, subsurface
    and low air bursts the cases that produce it (§9.01, p.&nbsp;388). A 1-kiloton fission
    explosion yields fission products whose activity is of the order of 10<sup>21</sup>
    disintegrations per second (§9.13, p.&nbsp;390), and early fallout consists of particles
    contaminated mainly, but not entirely, with fission products (§9.15, p.&nbsp;391). That is
    the manual's subject: one explosion, close in, on the ground.
  </p>

